WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.887
Two pieces make the check real.

2
00:00:01.887 --> 00:00:06.525
First, the code works out the claims the
workload's issuer will put in its token.

3
00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:10.778
For GitHub, Microsoft Learn's
trust page gives the formats:

4
00:00:10.778 --> 00:00:14.500
the repository and an
environment, a branch or a tag.

5
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.038
For Kubernetes, it's the service account.

6
00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:20.396
Anything it can't be sure
of, such as a pull request,

7
00:00:20.396 --> 00:00:23.838
has to be captured from a
real test run, not derived.

8
00:00:23.838 --> 00:00:25.899
Second, the exchange test.

9
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:31.335
It builds the token request from Microsoft
Learn's OAuth 2.0 client credentials flow

10
00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:34.286
page and sends it only to
a fake token endpoint,

11
00:00:34.286 --> 00:00:38.712
which matches exactly and
case-sensitively, the way Learn describes.

12
00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:42.600
It passes only if the named
credential is the one that matched.

13
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.463
Be clear about what that proves.

14
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:47.512
It shows a credential will
match the claims you recorded.

15
00:00:47.512 --> 00:00:50.495
Whether the real issuer
presents those claims,

16
00:00:50.495 --> 00:00:53.450
and what the real service
answers, is Lab 2's job.
